# vLLM-MLX Local Project Rules

## Project Context
This is a POC to prove vLLM with MLX backend is faster than standard MLX inference on MacBook unified memory. The goal is to benchmark and validate efficiency gains, then expose an OpenAI-compatible API for Cursor integration.

## Tech Stack
- Python 3.11+
- MLX / mlx-lm (Apple Silicon ML framework)
- FastAPI + Uvicorn (OpenAI-compatible API server)
- pytest / pytest-asyncio (TDD workflow)

## Development Workflow
- **Test-Driven Development**: Write tests first, then implement
- **Stage-based**: Complete each stage fully before moving to next
- **Benchmarks are tests**: Performance proofs are part of the test suite

## Code Style (from user rules)
- Avoid magic numbers, use named constants
- if statements must have braces {}
- Use descriptive variable names with camelCase
- Functions should be under 100 lines, single responsibility
- No more than 5 function parameters
- Early return pattern for error handling
- Comments every ~20 lines for key logic

## Memory Model
- 70% of available memory for model weights
- 30% of available memory for KV cache
- Minimum model: Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-4bit (~4GB)

## API Server
- Port: 52198 (rare localhost port)
- OpenAI-compatible: /v1/models, /v1/completions, /v1/chat/completions

## File Structure
```
src/vllm_mlx/
├── memory.py      # Stage 1: Memory detection
├── models.py      # Stage 1: Model registry + recommendation
├── engine.py      # Stage 2: vLLM+MLX core
├── benchmark.py   # Stage 3: Benchmark utilities
└── server.py      # Stage 4: OpenAI API server
```

## Testing
- tests/test_stage1_memory.py - Memory detection + model recommendation
- tests/test_stage2_engine.py - Model loading + inference
- tests/test_stage3_benchmarks.py - Performance proofs
- tests/test_stage4_cursor.py - OpenAI API E2E tests
